=== SURVEY (web source is THE spec) ===
web/src/components/ui/Select.tsx is a PURE presentational form select (a styled native <select>):
  props: options[{value,label,disabled?}], label?, help? (= Omit<HelpIconProps,'for'> & {for?}),
         error?, hint?, placeholder?, size? ('sm'|'md'|'lg'|'auto', default 'md'), id?, required?,
         disabled?, plus the controlled value / onChange (native select attrs).
  render decisions reproduced VERBATIM:
   - selectId = id || label?.toLowerCase().replace(/\s+/g, '-')
   - {label && <Label htmlFor={selectId} required>{label}</Label>}{help && <HelpIcon … for={help.for ?? selectId}/>}
   - {placeholder && <option value="">{placeholder}</option>}; options.map(<option value disabled>)
   - error ⇒ red border + aria-invalid; aria-describedby = error ? `${id}-error` : hint ? `${id}-hint` : undefined
   - {error && <p id=…-error>}; {hint && !error && <p id=…-hint>}
  i18n: the component resolves NONE of its own t() keys (label/error/hint/placeholder arrive already
        localized; form.required is delegated to <Label>, the help keys to <HelpIcon>) → anonymous
        surface, regions reproduced. Data sources: none (presentational). Charts/maps: none.
  Existing native: a thin `TSSelect` exists (Sources/Components/UI/Pickers.swift) but lacks parity
  (no error/hint/placeholder/help/sizes/described-by/a11y) → NOT reused; a parity surface is built.

=== REASONING (native composition, Apple HIG) ===
- Native peer of the web <select> = a SwiftUI `Menu` whose collapsed trigger is the styled field box
  and whose rows are the options (per-option `.disabled()` = web <option disabled>, a checkmark on
  the selected row). This reproduces the web disabled-option semantics + the controlled value, which
  a bare `Picker(.menu)` cannot express per-row.
- Sizes: web sizeClasses (sm/md/lg/auto) → SwiftUI `controlSize` (.small/.regular/.large) + token
  padding so the control scales natively + with Dynamic Type rather than baking fixed point sizes.
  Web 'auto' (ui_density-driven) folds to .regular — density is an app-level concern applied ABOVE
  this presentational surface, documented (not faked).
- Accessibility: the control's spoken name folds in the localized "required" (native peer of
  aria-required, which the web reads off the paired <Label>); the error/hint text is surfaced as the
  control's accessibilityHint (native peer of aria-describedby); the captions carry their element ids
  as accessibility identifiers; VoiceOver/Dynamic Type/reduce-motion honoured.
- Composition reuses the EXACT primitives the web composes: FormLabel (web <Label>) + HelpIcon (web
  <HelpIcon>), with help.for resolved against the control id by the projector (web help.for ?? selectId).
- Telemetry: view.opened emitted once per model instance (P1/S11), slug "Select".
- i18n facade owns only the native a11y leaves the SwiftUI control needs (the web <select> does not):
  select.empty (the "never a blank box" empty leaf), select.untitled (label-less spoken name),
  select.required (the aria-required suffix). All via the P1/S10 NSLocalizedString facade.

States reproduced — every REAL branch of the web source renders (no hidden surfaces):
  labelled / unlabelled · help affordance · unselected prompt · each option (disabled honoured) ·
  sizes sm/md/lg/auto · required · errored (red border + caption + invalid + described-by) ·
  hinted (muted caption, suppressed by an error) · the native "never a blank box" empty leaf
  (options empty + no prompt → disabled muted control with localized copy = the acceptance `empty`).
The generic data-feed leaf states loading / stale / offline DO NOT apply to a stateless, networkless
presentational primitive (there is nothing to fetch, age, or lose connectivity to); inventing such
chrome would fabricate states the source does not have (covenant 3/5). Same precedent as the sibling
presentational primitives Label (0218), HelpIcon (0215), CopyButton (0207). The error branch IS one
of the prompt's listed states and is reproduced; empty IS reproduced as the no-rows leaf.

